Skill create_ai_task {
  label: 'Create a scheduled AI task'
  description: "Use when the user wants an analysis to run on a schedule, such as a recurring report, digest, check or alert, even without saying “AI task”. Typical phrasings: “run revenue by category every Monday”, “automate our weekly category review”, “check every morning that the data landed”, “every Monday at 8am, email me which categories changed the most”. Do not use for a one-off answer, a send right now, or a change to an existing task (use edit_ai_task instead)."
  invocation: 'auto'
  disabled: H.git.is_production
  content: @md
    # Create a scheduled AI task

    Draft a task that runs unattended once published. Settle everything it needs with the user in this chat, then save it as a draft. Do not run the analysis or send anything. Work only from this conversation: earlier chats belong to other tasks, so open one only when the user points to it.

    ## Ask before deciding

    - Ask an `ask_user` card for each piece the request leaves vague. Decide only what is clear or has one standard answer (Monday–Sunday weeks, ties, the workspace timezone), and state it in the brief.
    - Every question is a card, never plain text or `#opt` buttons: a reply without a tool call ends the turn.
    - Ask each card as soon as its facts are ready, batching the cards that are ready together.
    - Cover only what the user asked for or picked. Add no comparison, ranking, top N, threshold or metric of your own.

    Write each card in business words, never field names. The question has at most 15 words. The recommended option comes first, marked "(recommended)", followed by at most 2 more. Each option has a label of at most 5 words and one short line with the fact that decides it (today's number, latest data, Endorsed), quoted exactly as the data returned it.

    ## The cards

    - **Time:** the day or clock time is missing or a range ("Monday morning", "weekly", "end of day"). "Morning" recommends 9:00 AM.
    - **Which data:** two or more current datasets fit. Each option shows what it covers, its latest data and whether it's Endorsed. Recommend the endorsed one.
    - **Rule:** a vague word that changes the result. Each option shows today's numbers, from the latest complete period against the one before.
      - Metric ("sales", "performing"): each candidate metric's total.
      - Ranking ("changed the most", "top" without a number): the top rows, such as "Top 5 by dollar change (recommended)".
      - Threshold ("a lot", "big", "worst"), asked as "What counts as <the word>?": 2–3 thresholds, each with how many groups it catches today and their values. Recommend the one that catches the clear outliers ("20% or more · 4 today: Mobiles −35.8%, Bathroom −34.3%, …"). "Worst" means a few rows by change, not by size.
    - **Send:** only when the request never mentions sending, asked after every other card: "Send this task's results each run?" with "Yes, send them" and "No, keep them in the task". Recommend neither. Yes hands over to `add_ai_task_delivery`. No keeps the result in the run record.

    ## Pick the source

    - A pinned dataset, model or dashboard, or a dataset the user names by its exact label, is the source when it has the fields and current data. Never swap in a look-alike, and never ask where its data lives.
    - Otherwise, find the datasets that have the fields the request needs, with each one's latest data date.
    - Data that ends before the latest complete period isn't current. Never offer or save such a dataset, and never shift the period to fit it. If the pinned one is stale, ask "<Name> <why it can't serve>. Find another source?" with "Find another source for me" (recommended) and "Use <name> anyway".
    - When nothing fits, ask "Where do you look at this number today?" with a dashboard name or "Not sure, pick for me".

    ## Shape the task

    - **Schedule:** one cadence (daily or one weekday) and one clock time, matching the period. A week-over-week check runs on Mondays, never daily. Never ask about the timezone.
    - **Rows:** every group, highest first, unless the user says top, most, biggest or worst.
    - **Anomalies:** only when the user asks for them ("anomalies", "unusual", "outliers"). The brief then gets the anomaly step from `write_ai_task_brief`. Never add it on your own.
    - **Delivery:** when the task emails, posts to Slack or alerts, `add_ai_task_delivery` settles the recipients, the condition and the attachment.

    Then write, save and report the draft with `write_ai_task_brief`.

    ## Examples

    > User: "Set up a revenue by category analysis every Monday morning."
    > Cards: Time first, Which data if two current datasets fit, Send last.

    > User: "Every Monday at 8am, email me which categories changed the most."
    > Cards: one Rule card ("Top 5 by dollar change (recommended)"). The time and the recipient are clear.

    > User: "Ping #sales if any category's revenue drops a lot week over week."
    > Cards: one threshold Rule card with today's counts. The channel is clear, so there is no Send card.
  ;;
}
